{#
   THE LOCATION PICKER — for screens that EDIT one location's rows.

   ⚠️ **It is no longer the control on the admin lists (S134h).** Those now show
   location as the first "Affiner" dropdown inside `_admin_filters.html.twig`.
   The review that settled the list format concluded a page gets exactly ONE row
   of one-click tiles, and it belongs to the facet the page is *about* — a
   machines list is about machines, not about which room they are in. This file
   survives for the one screen where picking a location is not a filter at all
   but the choice of which row is being written: `/admin/horaires`. Read the
   `allow_all` note below before reusing it anywhere else.

   ⚠️ **It filters what is shown. It is never an authorization control.**
   `VenueContext` resolves a *display* scope; the voters decide access separately.

   ⚠️ **It renders nothing on an install with one location (S130c).** A select
   whose only choices are "all locations" and the only location is a control
   that cannot change anything — and it was drawn on every admin list, above the
   filters, on every single-venue install. Both call sites used to ask for it
   unconditionally; the test lives here so neither can forget it.

   ⚠️ **It is a row of one-click tiles, not a dropdown (S130c).** Every other
   filter on these pages — categories, status, the tile groups — is one click on
   a labelled chip row. This one was a `<label>` + `<select>` + right-aligned
   paragraph in a full-width bar of its own, above the panel that holds the rest,
   so the one filter that scopes *everything* looked like it belonged to another
   screen and cost two clicks and a menu. Same markup as the others now:
   `ml-filter-group-label` + `ml-cats` + `ml-tile`.

   Parameters:
     venue_context      the array from `VenueContext::forRequest()` or `::single()`
     list_route         route the tiles link to
     list_route_params  route parameters, if it needs any
     list_query         current query string, re-emitted on every tile so changing
                        location does not silently drop search, facets or sort
     allow_all          include the "all locations" tile (default true). ⚠️ Screens
                        that **edit** venue-scoped rows must pass false: opening
                        hours are stored per venue, so "all" names no row to write,
                        and offering it is a control that cannot succeed.

   ⚠️ `page` is dropped from every tile. Narrowing to a location with fewer rows
   while staying on page 4 shows an empty list, which reads as "this location has
   nothing in it". The filter chips have always dropped it; the select never did.
#}
{% if venue_context.venues|length > 1 %}
    {% set carried = list_query|default({})|filter((value, key) => key not in ['location', 'page']) %}
    {% set base = list_route_params|default({}) %}
    <div class="ml-filter-group">
        <span class="ml-filter-group-label" id="venue-context-label">{{ 'venue_context.label'|trans }}</span>
        <nav class="ml-cats" aria-labelledby="venue-context-label">
            {# 🔴 **`allow_all|default(true)` was always true.** Twig's `default`
               fires on any *empty* value, not just an undefined one, and `false`
               is empty — so `allow_all: false` resolved to `true` and
               `/admin/horaires` has offered "all locations" since S131, the one
               option its own include comment says must not exist there. Choosing
               it does not fail: `VenueContext::single()` treats `all` as "not
               specified" and falls back to the default venue, so the operator
               edits the default venue's week while the URL says `location=all`.
               That is the silent-wrong-row failure S131 was written to prevent.
               `is defined` is the test that distinguishes the two. #}
            {% if allow_all is not defined or allow_all %}
                {% set on = venue_context.location == 'all' %}
                <a class="ml-tile{{ on ? ' is-on' : '' }}"
                   href="{{ path(list_route, base|merge(carried)|merge({location: 'all'})) }}"
                   {% if on %}aria-current="true"{% endif %}>
                    <span class="ml-tile-label">{{ 'venue_context.all'|trans }}</span>
                </a>
            {% endif %}
            {% for venue in venue_context.venues %}
                {% set on = venue_context.location == venue.slug %}
                <a class="ml-tile{{ on ? ' is-on' : '' }}"
                   href="{{ path(list_route, base|merge(carried)|merge({location: venue.slug})) }}"
                   {% if on %}aria-current="true"{% endif %}>
                    <span class="ml-tile-label">{{ venue.name }}</span>
                </a>
            {% endfor %}
        </nav>
        {# ⚠️ Shown only while a location is actually selected. The sentence
           answers a question that arises when someone narrows the view and
           wonders whether they have just changed what they are allowed to do;
           on "all locations" nothing is narrowed and it is a permanent line of
           reassurance about a filter that is not filtering. #}
        {% if venue_context.location != 'all' %}
            <p class="ml-filter-group-note">{{ 'venue_context.not_a_right'|trans }}</p>
        {% endif %}
    </div>
{% endif %}
